  "title": "a fork whose arms stagger: what levels them, and what it costs",
  "source": "plan-proposal",
  "source_detail": "Q1 of how-a-graph-reads; every count measured by running the real layout() over the committed graphs",
  "explanation": "A fork's arms stagger only when one arm is also reachable later in the flow, which is 8 of the 46 forks in the committed graphs. [The three answers that keep every arrow travelling downhill](#keeps-arrows-downhill) differ in what they spend to level the arms — nothing, most of the picture's height, or nothing because they change nothing — and the fourth spends the downhill rule itself. Leaves out the change to which face an arrow meets, which lands regardless and is what makes a stagger read as a fan instead of a sequence.",
